  "notes": "Def Jam singer-songwriter carrying Stargate's track-first European pop-R&B, 2006–2010. The research gives \"86–100 straight (backbeat 2&4) plus a 122–128 four-on-the-floor tail from 2008\"; the R&B band is the base with the mode at 93, the sourced tempo of the signature record, and the club tail is a different lane. Three drum deltas, all from the entry: \"16th hats continuous and even (no swing)\" is why `swing.amount` is 0.50 with `quantizeStrength` 0.94 and `velocityVar` 0.1 — the most even kit in this section, in a genre whose stated rule is *not* to tighten it; \"snare/clap flat on 2&4\" is a clap stack with `offGridMs` 0; and \"a rim or snap ornament on the offbeat 16ths\" is an offbeat-placed rim/snap perc lane at three to five hits a bar. Harmony is the identifying claim and the research states it as a contrast: \"4-chord diatonic cycles, not 2-chord vamps — `I–V–ii–IV` is the template\", so that family leads, major keys dominate against a minor-leaning lane, and `maxChords` is 4. \"Melody-first writing with 'killer hooks'; light tenor, narrow interval range, heavy multi-tracked stacks\" is a 0.86 step share with a 4–9 density; \"guitar or cello as countermelody\" is the counter at −12 sharing weight between `answer_lick` and `sustain_pad`. The 808 in this entry is a **kit** — \"an 808 kit used as colour under acoustic instruments\" — not a bass, so no `drums.bass808` block and a normal bass part. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are all velocity and density numbers and the melody register. ⚠ `session.keys` is part-sourced: the entry names `F#`, `Ab`, `Db`, `Bm`, and the remaining value `Bb`, together with all 5 weights, are INFERRED rather than read from the entry.",
